        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">1. Dielectric Polarization of Ice vs Liquid Water</h3>
        <p>
          The dielectric loss factor of solid hexagonal ice at microwave frequencies (2.45 GHz and 5.8 GHz) is exceptionally low:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] text-cyan-300">
          ε''_ice(f, T) ≈ 0.0015 – 0.0035 ≪ ε''_liquid ≈ 12.0 – 25.0
        </div>
        <p>
          This 10,000-fold contrast allows microwave electromagnetic energy to penetrate completely through frozen cryo-pellets without surface attenuation (penetration depth <span class="font-mono text-ink">D_p &gt; 1.5 m</span>). Volumetric dissipation directly supplies the massive latent heat of ice sublimation (<span class="font-mono text-ink">ΔH_sub = 2,838 kJ/kg</span>) straight to the receding ice core, completely eliminating the heat-transfer bottleneck of conventional freeze-drying.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">2. Atmospheric Non-Vacuum Sublimation Mechanics</h3>
        <p>
          Unlike traditional batch lyophilizers requiring complex high-vacuum chambers (0.01 – 0.1 mbar) and colossal chilled condenser coils, FM-ACFD operates at ambient atmospheric pressure (<span class="font-mono text-ink">P = 1.013 bar</span>).
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] text-indigo-300">
          J_sub = [k_g / (R · T)] · [P_sat(T_ice) - P_v,N2]   (where P_v,N2 &lt; 0.01 Pa)
        </div>
        <p>
          Sublimation is driven by a steep chemical potential gradient between the ice front vapor pressure and an ultra-dry recirculating nitrogen stream (<span class="font-mono text-ink">Dew Point &lt; -60°C</span>). Rapid fluidization keeps the boundary layer Reynolds number high (<span class="font-mono text-ink">Re &gt; 150</span>), sweeping away sublimated moisture within milliseconds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
